=== B. sentences of the NEW open text that are nowhere in the OLD open text ===

B1. [45 words] new wording
    It is very nearly a tie: there are two best places, one off Catalonia and one in Brittany, beside Nantes; the half centred on either holds about 81% of the world's land, and the first wins over the second by about the area of Estonia.

B2. [20 words] new wording
    Give every point of the globe a height: the share of the world's land in the half centred on it.

B3. [4 words] new wording
    That makes a landscape.

B4. [19 words] new wording
    The map shows it over western Europe: the darker the blue, the more land a half centred there holds.

B5. [50 words] new wording
    It holds 92.7% of the world's people, and on the population maps I used its best centre has moved: it is in the western Alps in every year I tried up to 2010, and near Berlin from 2020 on (the two later years I tried, 2025 and 2030, are projections).

B6. [11 words] new wording
    The difference is a sliver on each side of the world.

B7. [29 words] new wording
    The half centred near Berlin reaches Manila, Kuala Lumpur and Singapore; the half centred in the Alps reaches São Paulo, the south of Brazil and most of Ecuador instead.

B8. [14 words] was behind 'The half with the most people'
    In 1975 the South American sliver held more people, 41 million against 30 million.

B9. [11 words] was behind 'The half with the most people'
    By 2020 the Asian one did, 92 million against 87 million.

B10. [21 words] new wording
    The lead in 2020 is thin: between 2.6 and 4.9 million people, depending on the map, out of nearly 8,000 million.

B11. [28 words] new wording
    A map of that half had been published already: Bill Rankin's "Human Hemisphere" of 2015, for the people of the year 2000, with its centre in western Switzerland.

B12. [11 words] was behind 'The half with the most people'
    The two do not disagree, because they are for different years.

B13. [22 words] new wording
    A search of the web found nobody who had said that the centre has moved, which is not proof that nobody has.

B14. [20 words] was behind 'And woodpeckers'
    This study began with a few random words that had been sent to me, and one of them was woodpeckers.

B15. [33 words] new wording
    Going by where woodpeckers have been recorded, and counting a kind found on both sides of the cut in part, the half with the most kinds of woodpecker holds about 83% of them.

B16. [15 words] was behind 'And woodpeckers'
    It is centred in Gascony, in the south-west of France, give or take 300 km.

B17. [58 words] new wording
    And the move of the people's centre from the Alps to near Berlin is seen on only two sets of population maps, and those two start from the same censuses of about 2010; a third set, the only one that by its makers' account knows the censuses of about 2020, begins when the centre is already near Berlin.

B18. [39 words] new wording
    The three are not independent of one another, none of them gives a margin of error, and the lead of one half over the other in 2020 is between 2.6 and 4.9 million people out of nearly 8,000 million.

